2. Defendant Opposite Party 1 Md Ali filed the pre-emption Miscellaneous Case 80 of 1991 on 27-7-91 under section 24 of the Non-Agricultural Tenancy Act as a co-sharer on the basis of his right acquired by the deed being No.420 dated 28-1-1981 executed by the defendant No.2 Rabeya Khatun, mother of the defendant No.1 by way of Heba-bil-ewaj for pre-emption of the land covered by said deed dated 8-6-1991. The petitioner said Khaleda Rahman, thereafter instituted Title Suit 131 of 1993 for a declaration that the said Heba-bil-ewaj deed is void, because it has been effected in contravention of the mandatory provision of President&#39;&#39;s Order 142 of 1972 and for further declaration that the defendant pre-emptor is not a co-sharer in respect of &#39;&#39;Ka&#39;&#39; schedule land which is disputed in the pre-emption case.&nbsp; 

 3. The suit was contested by the defendant 1 only, in denial of the allegation of the plaintiff. His case is that provision of President&#39;&#39;s Order No. 142 of 1972 has been complied with while the document was registered and as such, it is a valid document and on that basis, he is legally co-sharer in the case land. Only to defeat the case of pre-emption, the suit has been filed. The said Title Suit after having been dismissed on contest by the trial Court, Title Appeal 273 of 1994 was taken by the plaintiff. On contest, the same also has been dismissed by the impugned judgment and decree now under challenge in this revision.&nbsp; 

 5. The said President&#39;&#39;s Order was made on 18th November 1972 and was published in the Bangladesh Gazette Extraordinary Part 3A dated 20th November 1972. Case of the plaintiffs is that the requirement of sub-article 4(c) and 4(d) have not been complied with when the questioned document was registered and, as such, it is a valid document.&nbsp; 

 13. A careful reading of the language and meaning of Article 4 of the said President&#39;&#39;s Order makes it very clear that the law makers were very much concerned to give protection against the transfer of property relating to the public interest of the new born Bangladesh at that time. By the questioned document mother has transferred some property to her son by way of Heba-bil-ewaj which is completely a private property and not at all connected with any government interest. So, it is held that only in case of transfer of government property the question of forfeiting the property arises and not in the case of transfer of a private property.&nbsp; 

 15. This court is sitting in a revision under section 115 of the Code of Civil Procedure. The vires of law, namely of the President&#39;&#39;s Order No.142 of 1972, has come in question before this court. In my view, this is not an appropriate forum to consider the vires or illegality as to the enactment itself which should be decided in an appropriate case and forum.&nbsp; 

 16. Very recently, in the case of Shahriar Rashid Khan vs Bangladesh, reported in 48 DLR 133 (High Court Division) question as to vires of the Indemnity Ordinance promulgated under Article 93(1) of the Constitution of Bangladesh, by the then President, came up for consideration in writ jurisdiction wherein a detailed and long judgment has been passed and the High Court Division has declared the Indemnity Ordinance is a void piece of law.&nbsp; 

 18. So the right which has been guaranteed by the Constitution whether can be taken away by a President&#39;&#39;s Order shall have to be decided in a proper forum as in the case of Indemnity Ordinance. I do not accept the view of the trial Court that it was a mere mistake of scribe. However it is exonerated. The appellate Court&#39;&#39;s opinion is however, correct which has found that some omissions have been committed in the affidavit attaching with the document, which are mere irregularities. There is some ambiguity, no doubt, in the law itself. 

 19. As to the interpretation of law, decisions in two important cases namely Dr Nurul Islam vs People&#39;&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh, 33 DLR (AD) 201 and Dr Neelima Ibrahim vs People&#39;&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh, reported in 32 DLR 201 may be looked into. I may also refer the decision in the case of Barada Prasanna vs Kobad Miah reported in 13 DLR 765 wherein it is held that when the words of law are ambiguous it should be interpreted liberally for the benefit of the appellant. The only material consideration in the present case is whether the executant of the document, rather, the donor, mother had any intention to transfer the property involving any public interest which is obviously not, and it is very much absent in this case. It is held that from the affidavit it is clear that the Articles 4(c) and 4(d) of the President&#39;&#39;s Order No.142 of 1972 do not come to embrace the omissions of the affidavit to nullify the validity of the document which is purely a transfer of a private property for private purpose. It is beyond question that the property which was transferred is either vested property or abandoned property or its transfer was restricted by any other law of encumbrance for the time being in force.&nbsp;
